# Target C — C74 pointwise Euclidean radial-flux frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_radial_flux_divergence_2026-08-31.md](../../reports/target_c_radial_flux_divergence_2026-08-31.md).

C72 gives averaged Euclidean radial transport and pointwise graph-layer stress. C74
adds a pointwise Euclidean radial-cut theorem.

For a diameter vertex `z`, put `r_v=|p_v-z|`. At every `v!=z`, define

`q_v=sum_(vj in C) alpha_vj[1-(r_j-r_v)^2]`.

The exact local equilibrium identity is

`sum_j alpha_vj(r_j-r_v)=q_v/(2r_v)-g_v dot n_v`,

where `n_v=(p_v-z)/r_v`. Thus every unloaded vertex has nonnegative signed radial
stress divergence.

For a regular radius `0<t<D`, orient every contact crossing `{r<t}` outward and set

`F_z(t)=sum_cross alpha_ij(r_j-r_i)`.

Summing the local identity gives the exact cut balance

`F_z(t)=J_z+sum_inside q_v/(2r_v)-sum_inside g_v dot n_v`,

where `J_z=sum_(zj in C)alpha_zj`.

All `z`-star diameter leaves lie at radius exactly `D`. If

`S_z=sum_(zk in H)beta_zk`, `delta_z=1-S_z`,

then loaded vertices strictly inside radius `D` can drain at most `2D delta_z` radial
reaction. Hence, for all D,

`F_z(t)>=J_z-2D delta_z`.

For `D>=20`, C18 puts all `z`-star reaction directions in one interval of width
`Theta_D<pi/3`. With `c_D=cos(Theta_D/2)`, this gives

`J_z>=|g_z|>=D c_D S_z`,

and therefore

`F_z(t)>=D[c_D S_z-2delta_z]`

for every `t<D`.

At the C66 center, `delta_z=O(D epsilon)`. Thus `D epsilon->0` implies

`inf_(0<t<D) F_z(t)/D >=0.90348...-o(1)`.

There is also an exact squared-radial coarea identity

`integral_0^D F_z(t)dt=D^2-Q_z`.

## Current near-zero normal form

Together C71-C74 force:

- one dominant port or a return component at `z`;
- `Omega(D)` stress on every pre-leaf graph layer;
- all but `O(D)` core vertices in clean degree-four strict-rhombus neighborhoods;
- at least `(0.90348-o(1))D` signed outward radial stress through every Euclidean
  radius below `D`.

The heavy carrier can no longer disappear into internal circulation. The remaining
escape is two-dimensional geometry supported by contacts with asymptotically
negligible weight in the chosen KKT certificate.

## Next target

Introduce a second resource that charges those light transverse contacts: reciprocal
width/area, KKT-certificate variation, or quantitative stability of the C71
return/separator branch.